  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">To steer the country towards the achievement of the TB Millennium Development Goals in line with the Global Stop TB Strategy - Kenya (01-Oct-2015-31-Dec-2017)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tuberculosis (TB) is one of the leading causes of illness and death in Kenya, having a disproportionate impact on women and children.  Since 1990, the absolute number of TB cases notified increased ten-fold in Kenya, as TB case notification rates for all cases has increased from 50 per 100,000 in 1990 to 329 per 100,000 people in 2008.  Kenya developed a medium-term TB control strategic plan covering the period of 2006 to 2010, which was modelled on World Health Organization (WHO) Stop TB strategy.  The strategic plan has steered the country towards the achievement of the global TB control targets of 70 percent case detection and an 85 percent treatment success rate.  However, a number of challenges still remain for Kenya to achieve the Millennium Development Goals in TB prevalence and mortality, including reducing the delays in TB diagnosis and improving monitoring and evaluation systems.  The Round 9 proposal is implemented by two Principal Recipients and aims to sustain and improve TB control gains in order to accelerate the reduction of TB incidence in Kenya.  The current grant trains community health workers on the use of nutritional assessment tools and provides nutritional support to TB patients who are moderately and severely malnourished.</narrative>
  </description>
